WEBVTT

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:16.580
Turner Home Entertainment presents A Dialogue, A Conversation with Carl Sagan and Ted Turner.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.370
Carl Sagan is one of the greatest minds on this planet.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:27.010
He's an accomplished scientist who has played a leading role in the Mariner, Viking, and Voyager expeditions to other planets.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.700
His research has enhanced our understanding.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.750
numerous aspects of the heavens.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.680
And he has brought his work to a general audience through such books as the Pulitzer Prize-winning

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:43.120
Dragons of Eden and his Emmy and Peabody award-winning series on television Cosmos.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:49.240
In addition, he has taken a strong stand in defense of this planet and its people by working

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:54.440
with his colleagues on research into the long-term effects of nuclear war and the destruction

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:55.420
of our environment.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.980
And he's made us aware of the tremendous dangers facing us if we

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.260
don't do something to halt the arms race. I'm grateful that he's here to share some of his

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.560
knowledge with us. Carl, pleasure to have you here this evening. Thank you, Ted. Thank you.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.860
We're destroying the ozone layer. We're heating up the earth. We're destroying our forests. We're

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.100
poisoning the groundwater with radioactive wastes and pesticides. How did we ever get in this mess

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:26.350
and is there any way out? How's that for a starter? That's a good question. Well, we got into the mess

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.760
by not paying attention and by businesses.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.960
usual. Humans have been on this planet for something like a million years. And for the

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:43.870
vast bulk of that time, things changed extremely slowly. The population increased very slowly. Our

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:49.520
technology increased, improved, but by very slow steps. And just recently, you know, this is what's

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.930
called an exponential. It's flat for a long time and then boom, you suddenly get a huge increase.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:59.970
There's increase in population, increase in technology, increase in pollution, increase

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.640
in our powers to disturb the environment, to change the planetary environment, but we're

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:11.120
the same old human beings as we were a thousand years ago and a hundred thousand years ago.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.820
Not much has changed with us.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:21.840
And so it's very hard for us to catch on that there's a new situation and we have to adapt

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:22.100
to it.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:26.430
On the other hand, that's one thing we humans are good at, adapting, figuring out.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:28.660
We're smart.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:29.740
That's our principle.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.860
advantage over all the other species. I mean, we're not faster, stronger, better diggers.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:41.170
We don't fly all by ourselves. What we do is figure out and build because of our hands.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:46.800
And so I think there's certainly a chance of getting out of this mess, but not by business

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.820
as usual, not by the idea that we shouldn't plan ahead, not by the idea that anybody can

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.560
do whatever the hell they want and it doesn't affect the environment. There has to be a new

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.960
way of looking at the world. A lot of those issues that you...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.020
that you raised are global issues, for example, global warming, the greenhouse effect.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:13.140
You put gases like carbon dioxide or CFCs, other greenhouse gases, into the atmosphere

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.790
over this country, they don't stay over that country.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:17.870
Those molecules don't have passports.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:19.920
They don't know about national sovereignty.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.100
That's something they never heard of.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.960
The atmospheric circulation spreads those gases all over the planet.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.610
And so what one country does affects all the other countries.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:29.960
The solution to the...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.640
these kinds of problems has to be that everybody on Earth works together. The industrialized

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.900
nations have the biggest responsibility because they're the biggest polluters. The United States

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.740
puts more CO2 in the atmosphere than any other nation. But Western Europe and the Soviet

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:49.440
Union and Japan and even the developing countries all make significant contributions. So there

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:55.570
has to be a new way of looking at the future, and that is that we're all humans, members

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:57.530
of the same species on one

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.980
fragile little planet. We're all in this

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.040
together and we have to work together.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.100
That's kind of the silver lining of these crises.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.500
They are forcing us to become a planetary species.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:14.920
Well, that's interesting and I certainly agree with that.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:25.650
Another problem that we're certainly all aware of is the bloated arsenals of nuclear weapons.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.970
We've had a tremendous thawing in relations between the...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.060
the two superpowers, the Soviets are going to be meeting with the Chinese, we seem to

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:42.740
have a real move away from war here on the planet and a move towards peace.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.860
Do you think we can get rid of these nuclear arsenals, and how do we go about doing it?

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.560
Well, you've got to ask what they're for.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:58.120
I mean, presumably neither the United States nor the Soviet Union really intends to blow

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.290
up the planet.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:05.990
destroy the global civilization. That's not what they're about. The professed function of the

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:12.560
nuclear weapons on each side is to prevent the other side from using their nuclear weapons. If

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:18.790
that's all it is, then we've got to ask, how many nuclear weapons do you need to do that? So,

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:24.600
for example, you could ask, how many cities are there on the planet Earth? Let's say a city has

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:29.980
100,000 people or more. You probably don't need more weapons than what's required to destroy

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.680
every city on earth. There's only 2,300 cities. So the United States by that

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:41.860
criteria only needs 2,300 nuclear weapons. Well we got more than 25,000, more than

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:45.420
ten times enough to destroy every city. But not all those cities are our enemies

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:49.300
either. No, no, I mean including our own cities. The Soviets say they're not our

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:53.320
enemies either. I mean I don't I don't know who who has enemies so bad that

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:56.940
they're willing to even think about dropping nuclear weapons on them. That's

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.080
right. I really hate somebody to do that.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:08.210
Suicidal. I mean, it's stupid even if you hate somebody. If they have nuclear weapons and you attack them, they're going to attack you.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:18.110
And so the thing is immensely stupid. If we were only concerned about deterrence, that's the magic word, to deter the other side from using their nuclear weapons,

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:29.890
then all you need is a tiny fraction of the present bloated, grotesque, and ruinous, including ruinous and cost, arsenals.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:36.630
A minimum deterrence that is absolutely safe, that is an invulnerable retaliatory capability,

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:41.280
could be done for a thousand nuclear weapons or a few hundred nuclear weapons.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:47.590
So, you see, what's happened recently is there's been this much-ballyhooed INF treaty,

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:53.420
Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces, which is terrific. I'm all for it.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:59.770
It lowers the arsenals by about 3%, and the nuclear warheads are being recycled there.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.730
even being gotten rid of.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:07.320
What we have to do is make vast, massive cuts in the arsenals on both sides, and nothing

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:09.100
short of that is going to make us safe.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:17.130
Well, do you think that there's the political will here in the United States?

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:22.610
The Soviets say, tell me, I'm sure they've told you, they're willing to get rid of them

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:28.480
over a reasonable time frame because we don't see any confrontations anymore.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:29.970
Do you think that, uh...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.430
This administration has the political will to join in with that?

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:35.330
Hard to tell.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:43.180
I mean, certainly the new factor, the stunning change in the world situation is the accession

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:46.020
of Mikhail Gorbachev to power in the Soviet Union.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:54.500
So it's not just that they're willing to have an INF agreement with intrusive inspection,

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:59.760
American inspectors on Soviet soil, but they're willing to have much more than that.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:07.240
made unilateral cuts in their conventional forces. For a year and a half, they made a

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:13.410
unilateral moratorium on nuclear weapons testing, inviting the United States to respond, to

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:20.630
join, to reciprocate. And so far, nothing. Carl, it's been a long time since I've gotten

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:26.050
updated or we've been updated on the status of the theory of nuclear winter. Could you

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:29.840
bring us up to date on that? Well, nuclear winter is the

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:37.540
predicted from physics calculations, cooling and darkening of the Earth following a nuclear

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:43.140
war. Basically what happens is mainly from the burning of cities, fine particles get put up

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:51.500
into the atmosphere, block sunlight, and so it gets darker and cooler. We did a little more than

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:57.470
five years ago a set of calculations showing that the effect was horrendous even for a small nuclear

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.980
that the burning of a hundred down

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:07.320
towns globally was enough to produce a hemisphere-wide global nuclear winter.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:16.780
And there have been a lot of debates on it because it has a set of very disturbing implications

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:18.060
about the nuclear arms race.

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:22.460
It challenges the fundamental ideas of nuclear deterrence.

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:25.050
And there's been a lot of fighting.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:28.250
What's happened now is that there's been a very nice convergence.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:29.890
some of the...

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:36.010
The prior claims that it's only a nuclear autumn, it's not so bad, it's not as bad as a nuclear winter,

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:42.460
turn out to have occurred from everybody not putting the same smoke at the same altitudes.

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:47.480
When everybody starts with the same starting conditions, you end up with the same very serious effects.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:53.200
So now that there's a convergence in the science, it's important to understand what the policy implications are.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:59.440
One policy implication, and it comes back to your question a minute ago about how many nuclear weapons is enough,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.480
is the idea that if Nation A makes a massive attack, for whatever reason, on Nation B,

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.950
Nation B doesn't do anything to defend itself or to retaliate.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:17.240
Nevertheless, the smoke that gets raised over Nation B circulates around the world,

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:21.800
covers Nation A, Nation A gets cold and dark and the agriculture fails,

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:28.010
and Nation A has destroyed itself by launching a nuclear war on Nation B.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:29.970
The main consequence of...

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:39.330
nuclear winter is massive agricultural failures. And many international study groups have now

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:48.050
concluded that the net result in mass starvation can account for many billions of lives. Well,

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:52.660
there's only five billion. How many is many? Well, hard to quantify, but you're absolutely right.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:56.960
It's a big fraction of the human community. And that's the long-term effect. And the prompt

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.800
effects, you know, you're going to kill many hundreds of millions, maybe one or two billion

00:10:59.800 --> 00:10:59.980
be.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.320
people in the direct consequence of a nuclear war.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:09.740
So it now appears that that nuclear war certainly will destroy the nations involved with it,

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:16.680
almost certainly will destroy the global civilization, and might just possibly destroy the human

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:17.060
species.

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:24.670
So it's another calibration of how serious the stakes are these days, how high the stakes

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:26.730
are because of our technology.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:29.660
nuclear war has put us in a position to

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.300
to utter devastation to our species.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.880
Putting greenhouse gases in the atmosphere promises,

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:40.930
if that's the right word, a global catastrophe,

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:47.600
not just destruction of farmland, flooding some places,

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:50.120
drought other places, rise in sea level,

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:52.620
inundation of coastal cities all over the planet.

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:54.060
That's serious stuff.

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:56.280
The depletion of the ozone layer

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:59.860
from these chlorofluorocarbon compounds.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.590
It lets more ultraviolet light from the sun down to the surface of the earth.

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:08.120
Skin cancer is a serious consequence.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:11.500
That's the one we hear mostly about, especially us light-skinned people.

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:16.180
Dark-skinned people are much better protected against it, but the more serious aspect of

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:21.840
it is that the ultraviolet light attacks the little one-celled plants that are at the

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:22.700
base of the food chain.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.080
You know, those are the guys that the next guys eat and the next guys, the next guys.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.190
and way up at the top of that ecological pyramid, there is us.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.830
We're ultimately eating the one cell plants that have been processed through lots of intermediate

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:35.610
plants and animals.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:44.870
And so again, we're messing around with the global environment in a very serious, very

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:46.050
stupid way.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:49.620
And we just have to get our technology in hand.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:55.060
It's not enough to say that corporations can do whatever they want as long as they make

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:55.520
a profit.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:58.540
Not if they're putting at risk people all over the world.

00:12:58.660 --> 00:12:59.080
They can't.

00:12:59.260 --> 00:12:59.960
That has to happen.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.060
be a new way of approaching this, and we can say that one nation can do what it wants within

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:09.450
its borders, because as I said before, what you do in one country's borders has consequences

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:10.550
all over the planet.

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:16.070
Well, we're gonna be talking about space exploration too, and not just all gloom and

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:21.340
doom, but while we're on the subject, what can the average person...I mean, I think

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:25.910
a lot of average people have heard a lot about this as the environmental and nuclear threat

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:29.870
have been pounded in and pounded in and CNN continue to walk.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:36.330
raise the alarm cry, but what can the average citizen do about it?

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:38.010
Because that's really...

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:42.620
A sense of hopelessness is not something that you want to convey or that I want to convey.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:49.220
What can the average citizen do to effect changes on the part of our government to make

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:50.360
the moves that are necessary?

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:52.540
See, I don't think it's hopeless at all.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:56.700
As I said before, humans are good at figuring stuff out, and we're good at change.

00:13:56.820 --> 00:13:57.820
We've had a lot of changes.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:13:59.720
What can the viewers of this program do?

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.520
do to make a difference in correcting these problems?

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:08.200
They can make sure that candidates who don't understand and aren't deeply committed to

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:14.870
ending and reversing the nuclear arms race, to stopping greenhouse warming, and to stopping

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:19.810
the depletion of the ozono-sphere, that those guys aren't, men and women, are not elected.

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:23.460
It's not enough to have a candidate say, I'm an environmentalist.

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:28.550
In what way are you an environmentalist, Mr. or Ms. Candidate?

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:29.970
Take a look at the...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:30.840
most recent election.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:35.900
There we had a candidate who said that he was an environmentalist.

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:43.060
He, in contrast to the previous eight years where we had an uncompromising anti-environmentalist

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:47.320
in the White House, okay, now there's a president, Mr. Bush, who at least acknowledges that there's

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:47.720
a problem.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:49.920
We didn't even know that from listening to Mr. Reagan.

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:57.180
But if you look at Mr. Bush's budget, we see not a hint of any real commitment to the environment.

00:14:57.180 --> 00:14:59.620
So, I say, in a democracy, that's...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.720
That's the most important thing a person can do.

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:03.260
There are a lot of other things a person can do.

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:04.750
You can plant trees.

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:08.050
Every individual can plant trees.

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:09.990
That's something that's very constructive.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:16.340
You can boycott industries which are irresponsible on global warming and on chlorofluorocarbons.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:20.620
There are many things people can do, but they have to understand the issue before they can

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:21.200
do those things.

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:22.600
Well, we just had an election.

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:25.280
We won't have another one for a couple of years.

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:29.970
Do you have any faith in writing letters to your congregation?

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.420
Congressmen and Senators, a lot of people believe that that has some effect.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:37.560
I think that it can have some effect.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.540
There's a multiplier effect because so few people do write.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:45.100
Those who do, the congressional staff says, hey, there must be ten or a hundred times as

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:47.560
many people who agree with this letter, but who haven't written to us.

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:51.860
So that's important, but that's not nearly enough.

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:53.690
People have to inform themselves.

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:58.370
They have to understand these issues, and this then relates to the whole problem of

00:15:59.550 --> 00:15:59.610
of Americans.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:04.220
not understanding science, not understanding mathematics, not being able to read, not know

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:08.920
geography. I mean, we're in very bad trouble if we don't understand the planet we're trying to save.

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:14.870
Carl, you've been involved with the space program for the last 30 years in a very major way. What

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:20.400
do you think are the greatest benefits that have accrued from our expenditures and our

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:28.980
exploration of space? There's a huge number of them. One is satellite communication. I mean,

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:29.520
this

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:37.890
This conversation is being broadcast all over the world, largely via communication satellites.

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:43.090
They sit up there, they go around the Earth as fast as the Earth rotates, so they hover over one spot.

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:47.580
And so you can send a message to one of them, and back it comes down to a different part of the Earth.

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:49.740
And it binds the Earth together.

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:52.220
It's a very powerful political fact.

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:57.190
There's a different way from what we were talking about before, in which technology is binding the Earth together.

00:16:57.910 --> 00:16:59.970
Another aspect that I think is tremendous.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:09.940
Tremendously important is those photographs of the earth alone in space fragile blue world in this vast

00:17:10.090 --> 00:17:17.530
Blackness this vacuum velvety vacuum of space and it's it's clear very thin atmosphere

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:23.320
It's so sensitive to the depredations of human beings you look at that and you say hey

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:29.920
That's only one little world. We don't have anywhere else to go. No other planet in the solar system is a suitable home for you

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.440
beings it's this world or nothing that's a very powerful perception then in in the particular

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:44.460
field that i that i'm involved with the exploration of planets by usually by robotic spacecraft

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:54.300
there we have opened up a universe of wonders we have looked close up at dozens of new worlds

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:59.910
worlds that we never saw before and unless we're so stupid as to destroy ourselves

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.600
there are going to be people exploring those worlds.

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:05.620
There are going to be human habitations on those worlds.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:08.480
We're going to be moving out into space in the next century.

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:14.020
And I'm fortunate enough to have played a role

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:17.320
in the first preliminary reconnaissance of the solar system.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:19.240
That's a terrifically exciting thing.

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:23.540
Then there's the fact that when you study these other worlds,

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:25.670
you learn about this one.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:26.850
It's a very important fact.

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:29.970
If you look at the individuals who...

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:36.660
played key roles in discovering the threat to the ozone layer, the increasing greenhouse

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:43.380
effect, nuclear winter. You find a very high preponderance of planetary scientists working

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:47.260
in there. People who've cut their teeth on other worlds and then come back to examine

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:51.840
this one. By comparing our world with other worlds, you can see a lot of things that can

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:56.480
go wrong. Venus, for example, has this immense greenhouse effect. Surface temperature is

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:59.970
hot enough to melt tin or lead. Anybody who says...

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.570
the greenhouse effect is just some fantasy. All I have to do is look at Venus, a very important

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:09.610
object lesson. And then there's one more, a lot more, but there's one more in particular

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:18.890
kind of advantage of space exploration that I would stress, or of space technology, and that is

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:25.170
military reconnaissance and treaty verification satellites. If you don't know what the other side

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:29.860
is doing, then the standard military prudence is to assume the worst.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.250
worst case. That means you then build up your armaments for the worst that they

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:38.250
possibly could do. They see that you're doing some of that, they do the same, and

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:41.250
you have a nuclear arms race which is absolutely catastrophic with our present

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:45.500
technology. The satellites tell you what's actually happening there to

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:50.890
remarkably high fidelity, so it calms the hotheads and paranoids on both sides,

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:55.820
and that's worth its weight in gold. So put all that together, plus weather

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:59.780
satellites, they save billions of dollars in crops every year, just

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.500
from knowing what bad weather is happening so foreigners can take precautions.

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:08.260
The space program has paid for itself many times over.

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:13.060
And none of this, of course, has to do with putting people up into space.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:16.610
There may be good reasons for doing that, historical reasons,

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:20.130
social reasons, reasons for building bridges with other countries.

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:24.770
I'm, for example, a strong advocate for a long-term joint US-Soviet program

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:27.760
to put Americans and Russians on Mars.

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:29.980
I think that would be wonderful for...

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:34.910
for joining the two countries together in a grand common endeavor on behalf of the human

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:38.750
species, benign high technology, reaching out to the next century.

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:47.000
And there are many other still unrealized practical applications of the space program.

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:50.480
We've not given it nearly the attention it deserves.

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:55.640
And especially in the last 10 or 15 years, the United States has been awful.

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:59.100
Since 1978, the U.S., which led

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:07.940
pioneered the exploration of the solar system, has not launched one spacecraft to the moon or planets in more than a decade.

00:21:08.440 --> 00:21:16.210
We have not launched one. We hope that there will be shortly, later this year, the end of that drought.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:28.860
But we have let the space program languish in the last 10 or 15 years, as we have let all sorts of social programs languish, as we have permitted...

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:35.400
The amount of poverty in children to increase before the end of this century, more than

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:38.000
half the kids in America may be below the poverty line.

00:21:38.570 --> 00:21:42.330
What kind of a future do we build for the country?

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:49.760
If we raise all these kids as disadvantaged, as unable to cope with the society, as resentful

00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:54.160
for the injustice served up to them, this is stupid.

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:59.440
And then what happened with the resources is they went into increasing budgets for arms,

00:21:59.540 --> 00:21:59.900
isn't that?

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.010
where the money went?

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:04.070
That and making rich people richer.

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:05.830
Those are the two places.

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:08.030
The thing about rich people and being one,

00:22:08.190 --> 00:22:09.770
I guess, is

00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:12.190
the money all gets reinvested. If you've got

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:14.130
money, you put it in a bank. The bank lends it

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:16.230
out to people to buy homes

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:17.650
or cars or whatever. But not poor people.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:20.320
But not poor people. It tends to stay

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:22.000
up at that highly

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:24.670
stratified, very... More people get employed

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:26.350
with capital formation

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:27.670
and so forth. Are you a socialist?

00:22:28.540 --> 00:22:29.980
I'm not sure what a socialist...

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:35.960
But I believe that the government has a responsibility to care for the people.

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:37.240
I'm not talking about dole.

00:22:38.050 --> 00:22:43.970
I'm talking about making people self-reliant, people able to take care of themselves.

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:47.010
There are countries which are perfectly able to do that.

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:49.290
The United States is an extremely rich country.

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:50.770
It's perfectly able to do that.

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:52.070
It chooses not to.

00:22:52.190 --> 00:22:53.810
It chooses to have homeless people.

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:54.470
It chooses...

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:57.750
We are 19th in the world in infant mortality.

00:22:58.480 --> 00:22:59.620
18 other countries.

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:05.000
save the lives of their babies better than we. How come? They just spend more money on

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:09.360
it. They care about their babies more than we care about ours. I think it's a disgrace.

00:23:10.220 --> 00:23:16.320
And this country has vast wealth. You just look at what something like Star Wars, the

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:20.900
money spent on Star Wars already spent $20 billion on it. If these guys are permitted

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:26.510
to go ahead, they will spend a trillion dollars on Star Wars. Think of what that money could

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:29.880
be used for to educate, to help.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:36.180
to bring people up to a sense of self-confidence, to improve not just the happiness of people

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:39.840
in America, but their economic standing, to improve the competitiveness of the United

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:41.600
States compared to other countries.

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:44.160
We are using money for the wrong stuff.

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:46.680
Paul, do you think time travel is possible?

00:23:49.580 --> 00:23:52.760
Well, first of all, we all are time travelers.

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:55.780
I mean, we travel into the future one year at a time, it takes a year to do it.

00:23:57.530 --> 00:23:59.830
according to Einstein's special relativity.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:06.940
which is a description of the way the world works. I mean, it's it's true. You can travel as far into the future as you want

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:10.960
if only you travel close enough to the speed of light. You can't travel at the speed of light.

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:12.880
You can't travel faster than the speed of light.

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:17.230
But there's nothing in physics that prevents you from traveling at, you know, 0.999

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.990
0.999 the speed of light.

00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:26.570
And then time for you slows down. And so you could travel a million years into the future and

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:29.980
and be perfectly okay. The question is could you ever get

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.650
And there is the present debate.

00:24:34.040 --> 00:24:35.940
Can you go backwards in time?

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:37.440
Is it permitted by physics?

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:39.020
Never mind, do we have the technology?

00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:40.400
Obviously, we don't have the technology.

00:24:40.660 --> 00:24:43.120
An interesting thing has happened lately.

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:47.510
I wrote a few years ago a novel called Contact.

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:53.670
And in it, I tried to imagine a physically reasonable way

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:56.590
to go quickly to some distant place

00:24:56.770 --> 00:24:58.870
without traveling close to the speed of light.

00:24:58.870 --> 00:24:59.470
And I asked her.

00:25:00.220 --> 00:25:07.360
friend of mine, Kip Thorne, who's a specialist in gravitation theory at Cal Tech, to tell

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:13.770
me the best way to do it. And that got him working with some of his students. And so they developed

00:25:13.770 --> 00:25:22.310
a most interesting theory of wormholes, so-called, sort of quick paths through space-time that

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:26.390
seemed to be permitted by the laws of physics, although it would require a very high advanced

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:29.960
technology, much more advanced than us, to do it. A part of that is...

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.060
is that time travel seems to be feasible.

00:25:32.980 --> 00:25:37.150
And so it is barely conceivable

00:25:37.240 --> 00:25:38.960
that a very advanced civilization

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:42.650
might be able to travel both into their far future

00:25:42.650 --> 00:25:44.860
and into their past.

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:48.080
It raises all kinds of funny causality problems.

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:49.340
What happens if you go back in time,

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:51.500
kill your grandparents before they gave birth

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:53.460
to your parents, then are you alive or dead?

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:54.100
What does it mean?

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:57.060
These are called causality problems,

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:59.860
and they are very...

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:05.220
So I would not say we understand this issue, but there's been some most interesting progress

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:07.520
recently because of the work of Kip Thorne.

00:26:08.130 --> 00:26:12.450
Well, you know, that really does raise some interesting possibilities because if the world

00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:16.940
is going to be environmentally degraded, you could take a few friends of yours and

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:21.100
we could go back in the past and try and see if we couldn't live with the Indians a couple

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:23.060
hundred years ago before the white man came.

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:28.830
The trouble is that to be able to do that, you need such an advanced technology that

00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:29.820
with that technology.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.800
you could solve our problem, or at least solve us, you may be more the problem than

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:35.460
the technology.

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:38.040
We also got to be the solution too.

00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:42.970
Well, let me ask you this question because we're covering so much ground in a relatively

00:26:42.970 --> 00:26:43.910
short period of time.

00:26:43.930 --> 00:26:49.070
You're an educator too, and there's so much criticism of the American educational system

00:26:49.070 --> 00:26:49.430
today.

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:53.590
Is that criticism valid, and what can we do to improve our educational system?

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:54.680
What needs to be done?

00:26:54.820 --> 00:26:55.740
It is valid.

00:26:55.740 --> 00:26:59.940
And you compare how American kids do compared to kids

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:03.440
the same age in other countries, and they do miserably.

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:09.020
I'm not talking just about kids in Japan or Germany or the Soviet Union.

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:18.740
Kids in Singapore, kids in Thailand do much better in science and technology and mathematics

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:20.820
than Americans of the same age.

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:22.760
Our kids are not working hard enough.

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:29.530
There is not an ethos of respect and admiration for learning, certainly not on television.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:36.540
E. N. aside, the amount of real intellectual content that appears on the mass media is very

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:36.820
little.

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:44.680
The amount of time kids work, the amount of homework, the salaries of teachers, the encouragement

00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:49.910
of really good teachers, all of that is in a desperately bad situation.

00:27:50.410 --> 00:27:58.270
I saw a study where high school kids in Texas were shown a Mercator projection map of the

00:27:58.270 --> 00:27:59.690
world, but with none of the nations.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:01.080
with their names in them.

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:05.840
A lot of those kids didn't know where their own country was, could not pick out the United

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:06.900
States on a world map.

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:12.610
Some ridiculous number of them, 25 percent or something, when asked where the Soviet

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:14.590
Union was, pointed to Central America.

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:15.570
What do we do about it?

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:18.230
How can we improve our educational system?

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:25.560
One thing you can do is you can put a lot more federal money towards improving the education

00:28:26.300 --> 00:28:29.940
in the schools, which is, of course, a state function as specified.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:30.680
in the Constitution.

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:35.620
The Constitution doesn't say that the federal government can't help to educate its own children

00:28:35.620 --> 00:28:39.260
so that the United States will be in reasonable shape in the future.

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:42.220
But don't we spend more money than any other country on education by far?

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:43.020
I don't think so.

00:28:43.080 --> 00:28:43.740
Not per capita.

00:28:44.140 --> 00:28:45.060
I don't think that's right.

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:48.250
And the ethic of hard work, the ethic of...

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:52.930
Now that money, money, I'm really questioning whether it's a total money problem.

00:28:53.170 --> 00:28:59.970
Ted, I know many cases of very bright men and women who are coming out...

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:04.960
of college who temperamentally would like to be teachers, but the amount of money being

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:09.900
offered by industry is so much larger than the amount of money that's offered as a teacher

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:14.210
that for financial reasons they go off in that direction.

00:29:14.410 --> 00:29:18.650
Do you think that problem could completely be solved with money?

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:22.300
Money alone won't do it at all, not at all.

00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:25.740
It requires a change in how seriously an attitude.

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:27.340
Parents have to stress.

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:29.740
Less watching of television.

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:33.200
Oh, very important for you to say that.

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:34.880
Well, I mean, I say it all the time.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:38.030
School boards and mass media.

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:38.910
I'll give you an example.

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:42.110
More emphasis at every level on improving education.

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:43.660
Absolutely.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:48.740
Every newspaper in America, with very few exceptions, has a daily astrology column.

00:29:49.330 --> 00:29:50.110
Astrology is bunk.

00:29:50.590 --> 00:29:51.410
Astrology is fraud.

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:54.440
How many of them have even a weekly science column?

00:29:54.930 --> 00:29:55.990
Why that disproportion?

00:29:56.800 --> 00:29:58.140
How much real science is there on...

00:29:58.140 --> 00:29:59.240
Well, they say they're giving them...

00:29:59.240 --> 00:29:59.820
That's what the debt.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.360
We're just giving the American people what they want.

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:03.900
And that, you know...

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:06.980
And we're dragging ourselves down and down and down

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:07.960
over the last 40 years.

00:30:08.120 --> 00:30:09.260
I know, I mean, I've seen it.

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:11.380
Carl, I don't think there's anybody on this planet

00:30:11.380 --> 00:30:14.200
that has better credentials to answer this next question

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:14.960
than you do.

00:30:15.740 --> 00:30:18.780
Do you think that there's life anywhere else in the universe?

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:22.320
Well, think is a strong word.

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:28.260
If you look at how many other worlds there are...

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:31.580
How many stars in the Milky Way galaxy?

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:36.160
How likely it is that most of them now, likely now, most of them have planets?

00:30:36.440 --> 00:30:38.350
How many other galaxies there are?

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:46.060
It seems the height of human arrogance to imagine that this planet is the only inhabited

00:30:46.060 --> 00:30:46.380
world.

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:50.460
But at the same time, we don't know if life elsewhere yet.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:56.700
We're just at the very earliest stages of exploration, and we've not found life anywhere

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:57.020
else.

00:30:57.820 --> 00:30:59.420
We've sent spacecraft.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:05.360
as I said before, to a wonderful, exquisite array of other worlds.

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:07.120
We've learned an enormous amount from them.

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:12.260
We find on some of them the chemicals necessary for the origin of life,

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:16.840
you know, the stirrings, the intimations of life, but no sign of life.

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:23.170
We've also used big radio telescopes to see if anybody is sending us a radio message.

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:28.280
And both of those efforts have not yet succeeded, so we haven't found life elsewhere.

00:31:29.210 --> 00:31:29.960
I would...

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:36.520
I would think a universe in which we are the only living things is much more incredible

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:40.280
than a universe just burgeoning, overflowing with life.

00:31:40.460 --> 00:31:41.460
But we can't be sure.

00:31:41.580 --> 00:31:42.620
It's an experimental question.

00:31:42.620 --> 00:31:44.420
It has to be addressed experimentally.

00:31:44.760 --> 00:31:50.570
And that's one of the reasons that I'm such an advocate for sending spacecraft to other

00:31:50.570 --> 00:31:55.930
worlds and for using large radio telescopes to listen for signals.

00:31:56.870 --> 00:31:59.970
Are we continuing to listen to those signals and monitoring more?

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:07.460
frequencies all the time to uh yeah the the uh the interesting thing about it is how cheap it is at

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:14.260
the present time the uh by far most sophisticated radio search program SETI it's called search for

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:20.840
extraterrestrial intelligence program on the planet is being sponsored and paid for by a private

00:32:20.840 --> 00:32:26.190
membership organization from members contributions it's called the planetary society it's past

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:28.860
Dean of California organization to happen to be the president of it.

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:45.570
But it's so cheap that we're able to do this eight million channel search, eight million separate frequency stations, if you like, scanning the northern skies to see if anything's coming our way.

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:47.690
Nothing's coming our way, our way yet.

00:32:49.030 --> 00:32:55.950
This issue seems to me to be a very fundamental because we are parochial.

00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:57.100
We're provincial.

00:32:57.300 --> 00:32:58.520
We're stuck on one planet.

00:32:58.620 --> 00:32:59.920
We know only one kind of life.

00:33:00.570 --> 00:33:03.340
And so we don't know what else is possible.

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:08.620
Also, if we talk about intelligent beings, we think a certain way.

00:33:09.220 --> 00:33:11.040
We think we've got a lot of stuff figured out.

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:18.820
But we're not positive that someone else, smarter than us, independently evolved on a planet of another star,

00:33:19.080 --> 00:33:20.700
might not look at the world in a different way.

00:33:20.800 --> 00:33:27.960
It would be a very sobering experience for us to compare what we think we know with what other guys smarter than us know.

00:33:28.480 --> 00:33:29.160
That's one...

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:29.840
one of them

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:32.020
of many important aspects to it.

00:33:32.570 --> 00:33:33.990
But imagine the other way.

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:40.000
We've now, as I keep saying, made a preliminary reconnaissance of most of the worlds in the

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:41.480
solar system, no sign of life.

00:33:42.010 --> 00:33:44.740
That suggests life doesn't come everywhere.

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:47.450
Life isn't all that easy to arise.

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:54.670
And it says something, therefore, about the rarity and preciousness of life on our planet.

00:33:55.200 --> 00:33:59.460
It's something that needs to be cherished, taken care of.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:06.780
So the flip side of not finding life elsewhere is a much greater respect for the life that's

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:06.980
here.

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:12.120
And here we are, destroying an acre of forest every second on the planet, destroying species

00:34:12.120 --> 00:34:19.990
left and right, and imperiling even ourselves, it's, I think, a useful perspective to recognize

00:34:19.990 --> 00:34:25.250
that life isn't all that easy to come by, that we have an obligation to preserve life

00:34:25.250 --> 00:34:25.810
on this planet.

00:34:25.970 --> 00:34:29.870
So there are many aspects of looking for life elsewhere that seems to be very important.

00:34:30.090 --> 00:34:31.630
Another one is our own origins.

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:37.840
How did we, us humans, us animals, us life on Earth, how did we get here?

00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:39.660
What do you think?

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:45.200
Do you think that there was the conventional concept of God, or do you think it just happened?

00:34:46.860 --> 00:34:48.740
Well, that's not the full range of possibilities.

00:34:48.780 --> 00:34:49.320
Well, that's true.

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:51.200
We could have come here from somewhere else.

00:34:51.580 --> 00:34:53.820
That's also possible, but...

00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:55.560
Where did we come from before that?

00:34:55.660 --> 00:34:56.060
Absolutely.

00:34:56.200 --> 00:34:58.020
So you want to watch out for the infinite regress.

00:34:58.240 --> 00:34:59.660
What's your personal opinion?

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:00.860
You know, put you on the spot a little bit.

00:35:00.860 --> 00:35:01.760
I'm a scientist.

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:03.760
So I say, I go where the evidence goes.

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:06.260
Not what I personally would like you to...

00:35:06.260 --> 00:35:07.840
What is the evidence as you interpret it?

00:35:07.880 --> 00:35:09.060
I would love to believe...

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:09.560
Your theory.

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:15.580
I would love to believe that there was a God who made us, who's looking out for us...

00:35:15.580 --> 00:35:16.180
And loves us.

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:16.820
Loves us.

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:17.420
We need love.

00:35:17.420 --> 00:35:18.160
Takes care of us.

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:20.290
Makes us intelligent.

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:21.290
Guides us.

00:35:21.490 --> 00:35:22.410
And keeps us.

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:23.250
Because we're in such a mess.

00:35:23.470 --> 00:35:24.350
We're doing things so wrong.

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:27.860
Then we would be relieved of the responsibility of taking care of ourselves.

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:29.980
The voice would come from up above and say...

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:34.020
Don't pollute the atmosphere, stop the chlorofluorocarbons, that's right.

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:36.620
But that does not seem to be the case.

00:35:36.640 --> 00:35:38.060
We have to solve our own problems.

00:35:38.140 --> 00:35:39.460
We have to solve our own problems.

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:44.090
Now on the question of the origin of life, there's been some very interesting progress

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:46.510
made on the early Earth.

00:35:46.770 --> 00:35:51.520
There are two different ways in which the stuff of life, the molecules that lead to life

00:35:51.610 --> 00:35:52.270
are made.

00:35:52.930 --> 00:35:58.100
One, it seems very clear, it was made in the primitive atmosphere, lightning, ultraviolet

00:35:58.100 --> 00:35:59.620
life falling on the earth, that kind of stuff.

00:36:00.410 --> 00:36:06.200
And the other way is, it fell from the skies, because at the time of the origin of the Earth,

00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:09.300
comets, a lot of debris was being swept up.

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:13.200
The solar system was a lot more traffic in it than there is now.

00:36:13.670 --> 00:36:17.050
And a lot of that debris, we know from the exploration of Halley's Comet, for example,

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:19.430
comets are very rich in organic matter.

00:36:19.510 --> 00:36:21.190
So the stuff of life was falling on the Earth.

00:36:21.290 --> 00:36:23.440
Now, is that the hand of God or not?

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:28.000
Well, if you believe in God, God established the physical laws of the universe.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:29.520
Chemistry is a...

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:34.160
consequence of physics, so all those molecules that led to life were made by God.

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:35.860
It's possible to believe that.

00:36:36.490 --> 00:36:41.730
I'm not opposed to that idea, but I just say there is no evidence for it.

00:36:41.940 --> 00:36:44.320
And where there's no evidence, I say keep an open mind.

00:36:44.660 --> 00:36:47.460
Don't commit yourself in the absence of compelling evidence.

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:53.210
Well, I think it's, you know, I agree with you that we're going to have to solve these

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:59.310
problems ourselves that we have today, and in historical religions.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:05.480
many wonderful, fine teachings, but none of them, they're all ancient by today's standard

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:12.220
pre-industrial revolution. And they didn't deal with the problems that we had today because when

00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:17.200
Christ and Mohammed and the other prophets were alive, they didn't have nuclear weapons. There was

00:37:17.200 --> 00:37:21.660
no problem with overpopulation or environmental problems, so they weren't even mentioned in the

00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:29.970
commandments and the Koran and so forth. Carl, do we have reason to be hoping

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:35.170
hopeful about the future, and what can we do to have a hopeful future?

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:42.050
Well, I think there's a broad range of things that the average person can do, especially

00:37:42.050 --> 00:37:47.030
in a democracy where, you know, at least in principle, the people control what the government

00:37:47.030 --> 00:37:47.570
does.

00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:51.030
The government is supposed to be working for the people, not the other way around.

00:37:51.410 --> 00:37:56.080
Also on a planet where things are moving very much more towards the idea that the people

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:59.830
are in control, the Soviet Union, fantastic changes.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.880
happening there. Not yet all the way. There's not a multi-party system. There's a lot of

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:10.210
things that they don't have, but the amount of progress there is absolutely enormous. Those

00:38:10.210 --> 00:38:18.480
are some signs of hope. Now, we talked before about writing to members of Congress and visiting

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:24.500
members of Congress, writing letters to newspapers, television stations when they do a program

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:29.980
that seems right. All of that is a useful application of the citizens' democratic prerogative.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:31.440
and has some leverage.

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:35.440
It doesn't have a huge amount of leverage, in my personal opinion,

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:37.800
because there are a lot of other factors.

00:38:39.200 --> 00:38:43.680
There's a concentration of control of the news media in the hands of the wealthy.

00:38:44.230 --> 00:38:46.330
The wealthy have a particular perspective.

00:38:46.530 --> 00:38:50.010
Yeah, I haven't seen any poor people owning newspapers or television networks lately.

00:38:50.380 --> 00:38:53.660
Well, that's a good point, and also it doesn't always happen.

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:56.580
Besides, if they did own them, they wouldn't be poor for very long.

00:38:57.200 --> 00:38:57.620
Yes.

00:38:57.970 --> 00:38:59.950
So that's

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:01.360
Well, that's one worry.

00:39:01.460 --> 00:39:08.370
Another worry is that great wealth has a lot of leverage in a lot of different ways.

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:14.750
And so rich people have a lot more control over what gets talked about, what gets seen,

00:39:14.870 --> 00:39:18.780
which opinions are acceptable and permissible than do poor people.

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:25.960
So just writing letters to newspapers or to members of Congress isn't going to do a

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:26.280
whole lot.

00:39:26.530 --> 00:39:28.420
Voting does a whole lot more.

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:29.960
Because the one thing that members of the House of Representatives

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.580
of Congress and members of the executive branch, I mean elected members of the executive branch,

00:39:33.740 --> 00:39:40.430
president and vice president, are very interested in is getting reelected. And if it were possible

00:39:40.430 --> 00:39:48.360
for people to clear away the electoral smokescreen, the stuff that's intended to cloud your mind,

00:39:48.520 --> 00:39:56.150
the stuff that's intended to make you look at subsidiary issues and not the fundamental issues,

00:39:56.520 --> 00:39:58.320
if people can do that

00:39:58.350 --> 00:39:59.870
if they can learn to think

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:05.960
straight, then they have an enormous leverage, an enormous amount of control.

00:40:06.530 --> 00:40:12.180
So I mean, in the previous administration, we had a president that thought it was good

00:40:12.180 --> 00:40:20.290
enough to label a very destabilizing, fundamentally first-strike weapon, the MX, as peacekeeper.

00:40:20.750 --> 00:40:26.950
As long as we called it peacekeeper, then the eyes close and everybody will be for it by

00:40:26.950 --> 00:40:27.950
naming it.

00:40:29.050 --> 00:40:29.970
We...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:35.280
We had an administration in which a transparent hoax, the strategic defense initiative, Star

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:42.180
Wars, intended, so we were told, to protect the civilian population, got enormous support

00:40:42.200 --> 00:40:47.880
because the argument was, listen, do you realize that we have no defense against the Soviet

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:47.980
Russia?

00:40:48.360 --> 00:40:50.020
The shield, the commercial with a little shield.

00:40:50.180 --> 00:40:50.940
Don't you want some protection?

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:52.000
Of course.

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:55.900
And of course people went for that because the right questions weren't asked.

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:58.860
And television, by the way, was full of...

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:07.540
graphics showing four Soviet warheads in a spiffy U.S. battle station going zz, zz, zz,

00:41:07.540 --> 00:41:07.680
zz.

00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:10.820
Like an electronic game, you know, at the airport.

00:41:11.340 --> 00:41:14.400
And the warheads went away, and that's what we were going to do until we could protect

00:41:14.400 --> 00:41:14.920
the United States.

00:41:15.570 --> 00:41:17.170
It was not the asking of hard questions.

00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:24.280
So the next step, and this is something Thomas Jefferson repeatedly stressed, is that if you

00:41:24.280 --> 00:41:29.750
want to exercise your democratic privileges and powers, you have to understand the situation.

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:33.530
And you have to understand it in a fairly deep way.

00:41:33.650 --> 00:41:40.680
You have to get beyond the rhetoric that politicians necessarily spread in order to calm everybody

00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:42.780
and to get reelected.

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:47.600
So an educated populace is absolutely essential.

00:41:47.720 --> 00:41:52.920
What people can do is to learn the actual facts to make sure that both sides of the issues

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:57.750
are expressed and that they...there's nothing, by the way, in these issues that the average

00:41:57.750 --> 00:41:58.710
person can't understand.

00:41:58.710 --> 00:41:59.970
The average person is.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:04.820
plenty smart enough to understand these issues, and most of those cases, almost all of them,

00:42:05.120 --> 00:42:08.200
which the government said if you have the facts we have, then you'd reach a different

00:42:08.200 --> 00:42:10.240
conclusion, that turns out to be bunk.

00:42:11.030 --> 00:42:12.990
Beyond that, there are a lot of other things people can do.

00:42:13.280 --> 00:42:14.620
They can demonstrate.

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:16.240
They can make mass rallies.

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:20.540
There is an American tradition of civil, nonviolent civil disobedience.

00:42:20.850 --> 00:42:21.650
It worked, too.

00:42:21.830 --> 00:42:22.930
It certainly worked.

00:42:23.030 --> 00:42:27.250
It helped do away with segregation, and it helped do away with the Vietnam War.

00:42:27.410 --> 00:42:27.830
That's right.

00:42:27.980 --> 00:42:29.980
And it seems to me that it is.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:31.160
It's very American.

00:42:31.240 --> 00:42:36.300
We ought to be marching for the environment and marching and continue to those courageous

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:41.720
souls that have marched against nuclear testing and against SDI.

00:42:42.820 --> 00:42:43.720
So for example...

00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:45.740
We've got to take our future into our own hands.

00:42:45.900 --> 00:42:46.500
Absolutely agree.

00:42:46.620 --> 00:42:49.950
Now, let me say another couple of words on...let me say one thing.

00:42:50.150 --> 00:42:55.720
For example, this next April, I think, there's going to be massive demonstrations at the

00:42:55.720 --> 00:42:59.970
Nevada Nuclear Test Site, in which my wife...

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:06.040
Andrea and I have gone there, been arrested before in nonviolent civil disobedience because

00:43:06.040 --> 00:43:07.920
they're the Soviets that stopped testing.

00:43:08.060 --> 00:43:11.360
They say, you guys stopped testing and we dismissed it.

00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:13.280
We refused to do it for a year and a half.

00:43:13.720 --> 00:43:16.390
They gave us, and they've said, you stop testing, we'll stop testing.

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:19.470
Testing is the main driver that drives the nuclear arms race.

00:43:19.610 --> 00:43:23.310
If you can't blow up nuclear weapons, you can't make the next generation of weapons.

00:43:23.570 --> 00:43:25.590
I'm in favor of the next generation of people.

00:43:25.870 --> 00:43:29.760
Now, I want to say there is a historical perspective.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:35.510
which suggests as bad as our problems are, the nuclear arms race, the environmental issues,

00:43:35.650 --> 00:43:38.450
as bad as all that is, there is some reason for hope.

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:42.780
There was once slavery on the planet. It is largely gone.

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:46.460
There was once human sacrifice. There was once the divine right of kings.

00:43:46.780 --> 00:43:48.960
There was lots of stuff that today we consider.

00:43:48.960 --> 00:43:49.640
The oppression of women.

00:43:50.230 --> 00:43:52.250
Absolutely. I'm sorry I didn't say that.

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:57.710
All of that is changing, and we can change these issues too,

00:43:57.730 --> 00:43:59.910
because our lives depend on it. We're smart.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:04.960
enough, we're dedicated enough to do it, but not by sitting on our duffs. We have to really work.

00:44:05.080 --> 00:44:09.380
Take action. Okay, well, Carl, we're out of time. It was a real pleasure to have you here.
